#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1441a2d6-6646-53cf-910b-640be6a3ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} {:answer/id #uuid "1d83c4d7-e29e-518f-8d81-fc678900ebe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263270} {:answer/id #uuid "3151187a-5967-53d5-ae4b-7ac589aab55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263421} {:answer/id #uuid "5d332831-6792-53d3-9b0e-7703fcddae9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263808} {:answer/id #uuid "970e8c1f-d7a6-5f66-9d87-f248bc2ef3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1441a2d6-6646-53cf-910b-640be6a3ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} :answer-field/id #uuid "2376d46b-16ff-5b21-93b2-796a7646d0b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3aa96b9c-802f-5254-a50f-7fd477588b2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130271" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a929-055747dc7f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406774} {:answer/id #uuid "82778c6a-7758-50ec-ad08-3720a9d490ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406777} {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc8cce-7237-5eb6-ac41-6a5901ea8c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406780} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf6508f-66f3-52c3-8fbf-477ac88383db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130719" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}})-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058f601c-efb8-52f4-ad80-c86475d38425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544099} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6d7d30-ea91-5f19-ad12-6c285ca3401e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544366} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4a37f5-406e-5615-bae5-8b7a2a141fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544512} {:answer/id #uuid "d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544542} {:answer/id #uuid "e3119854-8196-5825-a5c9-16cfb48b0d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d617b5ac-9818-5c3b-ba03-76a6cc05aeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ced3481-0c5c-5eca-982f-047cab2301fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" :question/math-academy-id "q-130722" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064b3549-4bf4-5a5c-b8dc-3968806ac3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263057} {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} {:answer/id #uuid "3a49bd46-cd0b-56cc-ad48-5f7f88b79995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263521} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab79447-463d-53f5-9910-346ef01f273b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "d106df35-59e0-5715-a37e-1c3233721325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1f3923-ed50-5a12-bc40-f829c5055015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a95a9cd4-1a5a-56cf-b216-e3ff43ec1b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-130724" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050f1921-3c44-5bbf-b7ea-35eab2c32786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263045} {:answer/id #uuid "6abc818b-9a7d-590c-806b-551c7bbe6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} {:answer/id #uuid "a6997c32-93ac-521d-9b24-002782474a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264426} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0b44e3-00a5-5e34-b4f1-766b6565f684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264496} {:answer/id #uuid "e957c96d-da16-5583-8c41-1dbbf28e3ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abc818b-9a7d-590c-806b-551c7bbe6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a084402-00ad-5a44-a106-2a26851c80b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d357ddc-9cee-5e05-b7e3-0ba7beb5d8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131115" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fcf330d-a9bc-5cf2-b434-a4434b6c918f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-1,1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263401} {:answer/id #uuid "402f1ba3-23be-5592-8f2a-ad27c9a309bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} {:answer/id #uuid "49bbfeb3-58d2-5e76-8091-57f95d4f4d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263633} {:answer/id #uuid "5b15af4c-f9dd-5de5-aecf-f59227dc3406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263785} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dfc3ea-bfe3-57da-b45d-efee09d91a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,1,-1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "402f1ba3-23be-5592-8f2a-ad27c9a309bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a93c22f-f072-55e4-a7e9-f76359ecbdf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23ceddb8-6aa1-5bf0-9ebf-c92f08b6f440" :question/math-academy-id "q-131127" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ at the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e70550-117c-5406-b1d0-1972e33a49a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321186} {:answer/id #uuid "2878fe71-6d72-558f-873e-69c5871866b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321464} {:answer/id #uuid "586da6b9-862c-580b-8d45-8410265668b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbac82-4e87-5e31-9d1f-ff934afb295a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321999} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7b79a-bbdb-5b22-bcb8-45d937f420aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586da6b9-862c-580b-8d45-8410265668b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0fe214-7371-518b-8883-59f70ef20780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "967a9051-3e85-5612-827f-8db0c0985ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36343" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}\\text{ }i-2y\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =\\frac{(2)}{2}\\,i-2(0)\\text{ }j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =i-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124556c0-f467-5318-900e-7da585d6554e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405134} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1353fa-aa2b-5093-bcea-f8bdbde39ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405136} {:answer/id #uuid "699346b8-439a-5fe0-9c40-fa77c142405e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405137} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce9fec0-c766-50ce-8550-9639ade8ba3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0a835b-df77-526f-b20f-c5410f491aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7c74ce4-3c4e-5ea4-ab88-89558065f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-36754" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2),$ with parameter $t=0$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} {:answer/id #uuid "a0817702-700e-5e7c-8da2-a35776ec98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385525} {:answer/id #uuid "569c2b8b-c5da-52e2-b41d-09fbec22be67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385526} {:answer/id #uuid "0109f1c6-5278-5400-a02b-474b0e4272e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385527} {:answer/id #uuid "92ed1ce6-3824-5dec-a381-53a6e97e1d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5decc890-1d01-5ef2-9154-9ed30e0d6582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385524} :answer-field/id #uuid "f683d60b-f987-566f-8e73-598501a40b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a6c1cae-376e-5dfa-b07c-5886b3b91c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d40bef-5cc1-533e-a776-8178968f8226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373127} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5e25f1-f9d6-5abd-be1b-a979b00ae356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373128} {:answer/id #uuid "f370372e-718e-527d-b206-115386ee2633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3b4fbb-9302-5aaa-8ef1-476b737ddbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373130} {:answer/id #uuid "a9003cf3-3b04-543a-a41c-c583ef479eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f370372e-718e-527d-b206-115386ee2633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09f18f3-dc2a-5860-86fe-1086c3a20b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03866640-0a40-5a70-8301-d2aa021aa524" :question/math-academy-id "q-71394" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,4).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}(4)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,4)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-0)\\frac{1}{2}-(y-4)0 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0eea45-e863-58d6-862a-727382209bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398949} {:answer/id #uuid "f143a8ac-50ee-51ef-b268-61d02a927238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398950} {:answer/id #uuid "997b1e10-3a89-5f14-bd4d-db6ba1a97e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398951} {:answer/id #uuid "da9bac7a-0578-5611-970c-aaa5ef3c56ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398952} {:answer/id #uuid "b37aff02-8b26-5092-a1c6-e4bfb88a16b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b37aff02-8b26-5092-a1c6-e4bfb88a16b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b666e0-c6c9-5c9c-b88e-9912d89e0283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0863b6f-83a0-5fff-bd6b-d5b930ec7673" :question/math-academy-id "q-71396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd5310c-dfc2-5a7d-bac5-5825f5064da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406775} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} {:answer/id #uuid "cd07008c-2d5e-5372-aa7f-53d2714271f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406778} {:answer/id #uuid "e415ebda-6949-5999-a629-48685dbda1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406781} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a7f1a1-3119-55e9-ab80-51ececd277a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f78fc66-8357-5b97-ab1a-5d79ee0e7c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077d2dca-c9d8-5043-92cb-18878e5ddc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-71397" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b6b69-1258-52ca-92cb-84d166055cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336344} {:answer/id #uuid "8847c9cc-20d2-574c-a780-1b2f9a38d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260} {:answer/id #uuid "9b429560-704b-5928-a704-fbc5ba05c495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337439} {:answer/id #uuid "edd7d436-4244-565c-b127-7c866996e6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338108} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dcdca1-0652-5268-9a5c-8bdbc0a1e227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8847c9cc-20d2-574c-a780-1b2f9a38d565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337260} :answer-field/id #uuid "b348b668-7c03-56ec-b406-92e416034172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7acbac43-bc81-5177-9def-8ee007ede43b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71404" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367ab4f2-93bc-5938-9462-dc0e018dd590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496776} {:answer/id #uuid "399e3797-afa8-52bc-9d91-1f755a50f199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496783} {:answer/id #uuid "d59ec8f6-4eba-53b8-a3b8-5a5f6ce7e72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497104} {:answer/id #uuid "db86fc5b-7216-54fe-8379-494c5e62c9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497123} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497200} :answer-field/id #uuid "631be222-c76c-5ee5-9b25-874887015acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71406" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd12d1d6-f236-57c3-bd6d-b0a476bef5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373141} {:answer/id #uuid "e0818182-cafe-552e-a4a0-98b0c1314881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z+1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373142} {:answer/id #uuid "df5c957f-327c-59b5-a275-854f61c6c1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y-1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373143} {:answer/id #uuid "4643467a-d570-58d2-ac66-45f1f209b1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373144} {:answer/id #uuid "c777726a-c485-5a89-bead-338bff4761cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c777726a-c485-5a89-bead-338bff4761cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373145} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f112b8-6ada-53ff-b6b3-be00d57b76b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d41ffa1d-b743-559f-b433-9851a75bb790" :question/math-academy-id "q-71408" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ at the point $(-1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1,1) & =2(-1)\\text{ }i+2(-1)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-2\\,i-2\\,j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ x+1=y+1=\\frac{z-1}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
